> [!NOTE]
> **Also covers GHSA-5w7f-g655-933c (PAR `request_uri`)**, consolidated here: same root cause, same reporter, same fix. Concurrent requests can redeem one authorization code, or complete authorization with one pushed `request_uri`, more than once.
>
> **Severity reconciled to Medium.** Every variant needs the attacker to already hold the single-use artifact: PKCE is required for all clients except `client_secret_basic`, so a code is redeemable only with its verifier or the client secret, and a pushed `request_uri` is completed only through the victim's authenticated session. What the race adds is a second redemption that evades single-use and replay detection.

---

### Summary
A Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) race condition in Logto's OIDC database adapter allows an attacker to bypass the single-use restriction of OAuth 2.0 Authorization Codes. By sending concurrent token exchange requests, an attacker can redeem a single intercepted authorization code multiple times, resulting in the issuance of multiple distinct access and refresh tokens bound to the victim's identity. This violates RFC 6749 §4.1.2.

### Details
The vulnerability stems from a non-atomic check-then-consume pattern in the OIDC provider's grant handler and Logto's Postgres adapter. 

1. In the grant handler (`authorization_code.js`), the code checks if `code.consumed` is true (based on a prior `SELECT` query). If false, it calls `code.consume()`.
2. In Logto's adapter (`packages/core/src/queries/oidc-model-instance.ts:167-174`), `consumeInstanceById` executes an `UPDATE` statement without a Compare-And-Swap (CAS) guard:
```typescript
const consumeInstanceById = async (modelName: string, id: string) => {
  await pool.query(sql`
    update ${table}
    set ${fields.consumedAt}=${convertToTimestamp()}
    where ${fields.modelName}=${modelName}
    and ${fields.id}=${id}
    // MISSING: AND ${fields.consumedAt} IS NULL
  `);
};
```
Under high concurrency, multiple threads can pass the JS-level check before any `UPDATE` commits. Because the `UPDATE` is idempotent and lacks a row-level lock or CAS condition, all concurrent requests succeed, minting distinct tokens for the same code.

### PoC

1. Victim authenticates and obtains an authorization code via the standard OIDC flow.
2. Attacker intercepts the authorization code (e.g., via malicious browser extension, referer leakage, or compromised native app).
3. Attacker uses a script to send 8 concurrent `POST /oidc/token` requests using the same code and PKCE verifier, synchronized via a threading barrier.
4. Observe: 2 to 3 requests return HTTP 200 with distinct `access_token`, `refresh_token`, and `id_token` values. All minted tokens carry the victim's `sub` claim.

### Impact
Any attacker who can intercept an authorization code can multiply their access by racing the token exchange. This defeats the single-use guarantee, allowing the attacker to establish multiple concurrent sessions as the victim, bypassing downstream rate-limiting or single-session enforcement mechanisms.
